.	http://purl.oclc.org/NET/cmcs/schema/:hasConfigurations		<rdf:Bag xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" > <rdf:li><rdf:href xlink:href="Parameters/chemsys.mvm" xlink:type="simple"></rdf:href></rdf:li><rdf:li><rdf:href xlink:href="Parameters/BasisSet.ecce_basisset" xlink:type="simple"></rdf:href></rdf:li><rdf:li><rdf:href xlink:href="Parameters/SetupParams" xlink:type="simple"></rdf:href></rdf:li></rdf:Bag>
.	http://www.emsl.pnl.gov/ecce:annotation		ECCE-QM, B3LYP/6-31G*, relaxed scan of the O=C-N-C torsion of N-methylacetamide, 0 to 360 degrees in 25 points. This calculation shows the rotation barrier of the amide bond, which has partial double-bond character: the planar trans amide at 0 and 360 degrees is the minimum, the cis amide at 180 degrees is 10.6 kJ/mol above it, and the energy rises steeply on twisting. The curve jumps from 92.0 kJ/mol at 120 degrees to 20.3 kJ/mol at 135 degrees, and again from 76.2 kJ/mol at 300 degrees to 20.4 kJ/mol at 315 degrees: a relaxed scan follows one continuous path, and the nitrogen stays pyramidal on the higher-energy branch until it flips. Compare nma_scan_rev, which runs the other way (hysteresis); the rotation barrier is below both curves.
.	http://www.emsl.pnl.gov/ecce:application		ECCE-QM
